          {
            "heading": "Part A: Inclined Plane (Ramp)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Inclined plane (dhalaan)",
            "body": "### How an Inclined Plane Works\n\nAn **inclined plane** is a flat, rigid surface set at a slope between a lower level and an elevated level. Instead of lifting a heavy load of weight $L$ vertically through a height $h$, we push or pull the load along the slanted ramp surface of length $l$.\n\nA longer, gentler incline creates an extended path, but dramatically reduces the required pushing force.\n\n```\n                              ╱│ ← Truck flatbed floor (height h)\n                            ╱  │\n               l (plank)  ╱    │\n                        ╱      │ h (vertical rise)\n                      ╱        │\n                    ╱          │\n ─────────────────╱────────────┴─────────────\n  Ground\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.1** – An inclined plane: The slanting length of the ramp is $l$, and the direct vertical elevation is $h$.\n\n### Ideal Mechanical Advantage: MA = l / h\n\nIn an ideal (frictionless) inclined plane, the mechanical **work input equals the work output**:\n- **Work Input:** Effort $E$ applied parallel to the incline acting over the slanting length $l$:\n  $$\\text{Work Input} = E \\times l$$\n- **Work Output:** Load $L$ elevated through the vertical height $h$:\n  $$\\text{Work Output} = L \\times h$$\n\nEquating input and output for an ideal machine:\n$$E \\times l = L \\times h$$\n\nDividing both sides by $E \\times h$:\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{Ideal MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{Effort}} = \\frac{l}{h}}$$\n\nFurthermore, because the effort moves along the incline through distance $l$ while the load ascends vertically through height $h$:\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{VR} = \\frac{d_E}{d_L} = \\frac{l}{h}}$$\n\nIn an ideal inclined plane, $\\text{MA} = \\text{VR} = l / h$.\n\n*Note on Nomenclature:* Here, $l$ is strictly the **slanted length along the ramp**, and $h$ is strictly the **vertical height**. Neither trigonometry nor angles are required when $l$ and $h$ are directly known.\n\n### Resolving Sophia's Question with Numbers\n\nLet us compare the two planks used by Mr. Sterling for the $1200\\text{-N}$ drum ($h = 1\\text{ m}$):\n\n**Plank 1 (Short, Steep Incline):** $l = 2\\text{ m}$, $h = 1\\text{ m}$, $\\text{Load} = 1200\\text{ N}$.\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{2\\text{ m}}{1\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{2}$\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{1200\\text{ N}}{2} = \\mathbf{600\\text{ N}}$\n- $\\text{Work Input} = E \\times l = 600\\text{ N} \\times 2\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{1200\\text{ J}}$\n\n**Plank 2 (Long, Gentle Incline):** $l = 4\\text{ m}$, $h = 1\\text{ m}$, $\\text{Load} = 1200\\text{ N}$.\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{4\\text{ m}}{1\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{4}$\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{1200\\text{ N}}{4} = \\mathbf{300\\text{ N}}$\n- $\\text{Work Input} = E \\times l = 300\\text{ N} \\times 4\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{1200\\text{ J}}$\n\n**Direct Vertical Lift (No Machine):**\n- $\\text{Work} = \\text{Load} \\times h = 1200\\text{ N} \\times 1\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{1200\\text{ J}}$\n\n*Verification:* In all three cases, the mechanical work required is exactly $1200\\text{ J}$ ✅.\n\nMr. Sterling explained: \"You see, Sophia? The longer ramp halved the required pushing force from $600\\text{ N}$ down to $300\\text{ N}$, but it doubled the distance from $2\\text{ m}$ to $4\\text{ m}$. In an ideal world, the total mechanical work is identical. **We traded distance for force.**\"\n\n### Real Inclined Planes and Friction\n\nIn reality, rolling or sliding friction between the drum and the plank opposes motion. The worker must exert extra effort to overcome both gravity and friction:\n$$\\text{Real Effort} > \\frac{L \\times h}{l}$$\nConsequently, $\\text{Real MA} < \\text{VR}$, and efficiency $\\eta = (\\text{MA} / \\text{VR}) \\times 100\\% < 100\\%$.\n\n### Common Applications of the Inclined Plane\n\n- **Warehouse and Vehicle Ramps:** Allowing hand trucks to load flatbeds smoothly.\n- **Children's Playground Slides:** A smooth incline trading vertical fall for a safe, sloped descent.\n- **Mountain Roads with Switchbacks (Hairpin Bends):** Rather than climbing straight up a mountain wall (steep incline requiring excessive engine force), roads wind gently back and forth. The extended road length provides a large $l$, reducing the gradient and force required.\n- **Staircases:** A segmented inclined plane dividing vertical rise into comfortable horizontal steps.\n- **Wheelchair Accessibility Ramps:** Gentle slopes ($l / h \\ge 12$) enabling wheelchair users to ascend independently.",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Dhalaan kya karti hai\n\n**Inclined plane** ek **tirchhi sapaat surface** hai jiska ek sira neeche aur doosra sira ooncha hota hai. Load ko seedha **h** unchai tak uthane ke bajaye hum use **l** lambai ki dhalaan par chadhate hain. Dhalaan lambi aur halki hogi to rasta lamba, par force kam.\n\n```\n                              ╱│ ← truck ka floor (ooncha sira)\n                            ╱  │\n               l (plank)  ╱    │\n                        ╱      │ h\n                      ╱        │\n                    ╱          │\n ─────────────────╱────────────┴─────────────\n  zameen\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.1** – Plank ki lambai l hai (slanting) aur truck ke floor ki seedhi unchai h hai.\n\n### Ideal MA = l ÷ h\n\nIdeal (friction-free) dhalaan mein **work input = work output**. Hum effort ko dhalaan ke saath-saath l tak chalate hain, aur load seedhe h tak upar jaata hai.\n\n- Work input = effort × l\n- Work output = load × h\n\nDono barabar:\n\n> **effort × l = load × h**\n\nDono taraf ko effort × h se baantne par:\n\n> **MA = load ÷ effort = l ÷ h**\n\nAur effort l distance chalta hai jabki load h distance upar jaata hai, to **VR = l ÷ h** bhi hai. Isliye ideal incline mein **MA = VR = l ÷ h**.\n\nEk zaruri baat: yahan **l = dhalaan ki lambai** (slanting) aur **h = seedhi unchai** hai. Dono ko mat mila dena. Is topic mein hum kisi angle ka istemal nahi karenge, sirf l aur h.\n\n### Ab Zeenat ka sawal solve karte hain\n\n**Plank 1 (chhoti, steep):** l = 2 m, h = 1 m, load = 1200 N.\n\n- MA = l ÷ h = 2 m ÷ 1 m = **2**\n- Effort = load ÷ MA = 1200 N ÷ 2 = **600 N**\n\n**Plank 2 (lambi, gentle):** l = 4 m, h = 1 m, load = 1200 N.\n\n- MA = 4 m ÷ 1 m = **4**\n- Effort = 1200 N ÷ 4 = **300 N**\n\nAb **work** dekhte hain:\n\n- Plank 1: effort × l = 600 N × 2 m = **1200 J**\n- Plank 2: effort × l = 300 N × 4 m = **1200 J**\n- Seedha uthane par: load × h = 1200 N × 1 m = **1200 J**\n\n**Jaanch:** teeno jagah 1200 J ✅. Lambi plank ne **zor aadha kar diya, par rasta double ho gaya**. Kaam wahi ka wahi raha (ideal case mein).\n\n> Mr. Shaikh ne kaha, \"Dekha Zeenat? Lambi plank se zor kam hua, kaam nahi. Jo force mein bachaya, wo doori mein chuka diya.\"\n\nTopic 1 mein Mr. Sathe ke 1000 N ke drum ke liye kaha tha ki ramp par \"kaafi kam effort\" lagta hai. Agar unki ramp bhi l = 4 m, h = 1 m ki ho, to ideal effort = 1000 N ÷ 4 = **250 N**, yaani seedha uthane ka chautha hissa.\n\n### Real incline: friction aata hai\n\nAsli plank par friction hota hai, isliye drum ko dhakelne mein **ideal se zyada** effort lagta hai. Tab **work output < work input**, aur machine ki **efficiency (η) 100% se kam** hoti hai. Efficiency ka formula Topic 2 mein dekha tha: **η = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%**.\n\n### Dhalaan kahan-kahan milti hai\n\n- **Ramp** (truck ka, godown ka, flyover ka)\n- Park ki **slide** (children's slide)\n- Pahadi **sadak**, jo seedhi chadhne ke bajaye **hairpin bends** (U-shaped mod) leti hui lambi ghoomti hai: lambi sadak, halki dhalaan\n- **Seedhiyan (stairs)**: ye bhi ek tarah ki dhalaan hain jinmein dhalaan ko kadmon mein kaat diya gaya hai\n- **Wheelchair ka ramp**: gentle ramp jisse kam effort mein chadha ja sake"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Wedge",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Wedge (fanna)",
            "body": "A **wedge** is a portable device formed by joining **two inclined planes back-to-back**. While an inclined plane remains stationary while a load slides up it, a wedge is **driven directly into or under an object** to separate, split, or hold it in place.\n\n```\n          Effort F (e.g., hammer blow)\n                ↓\n          ┌─────────┐   ← Thickness t (broad driving head)\n           ╲       ╱\n            ╲     ╱   ← Slanting slope length l\n             ╲   ╱\n              ╲ ╱\n               V   ← Sharp penetrating edge\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.2** – A wedge: The two slanting sides represent two inclined planes of length $l$ joined back-to-back, with a total head thickness $t$.\n\nFor an ideal wedge:\n\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{Ideal MA} = \\frac{\\text{Length of Slanted Side } l}{\\text{Thickness of Head } t} = \\frac{l}{t}}$$\n\nA long, thin, razor-sharp wedge has a large ratio of $l / t$, providing a massive mechanical advantage that easily overcomes the intense cohesive strength of solid wood or metal.\n\n**Everyday Examples of Wedges:**\n- **Woodchopping Axe and Splitting Maul:** Splits logs by converting a downward axe strike into powerful lateral spreading forces.\n- **Chisels and Knives:** The cutting edge is ground into a fine wedge.\n- **Nails and Needles:** Taper to a sharp point, pushing timber or fabric fibers aside.\n- **Doorstops:** A tapered rubber wedge wedged beneath a door converts horizontal door pressure into high vertical friction against the floor.\n- **Agricultural Plow:** A hardened steel wedge cutting and turning agricultural soil.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Wedge** do inclined planes ko **peeth se peeth jodkar** bana hota hai. Ye kisi cheez ke beech mein **ghus kar use baant** deta hai. Hum wedge ko dhakelte hain (effort), aur wo cheez ko dono taraf kholta hai (load, side ka force).\n\n```\n          effort (hathoda)\n               ↓\n          ┌─────────┐   ← motai t (upar ka chauda sira)\n           ╲       ╱\n            ╲     ╱   ← l = slope ki lambai\n             ╲   ╱\n              ╲ ╱\n               V   ← nuka (neeche)\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.2** – Wedge ke do slanting side dono taraf ki dhalaan hain (har ek ki lambai l), aur upar ki motai t hai.\n\nIdeal wedge ke liye:\n\n> **MA = slope ki lambai l ÷ motai t**\n\nJitna patla aur nukila wedge, utna bada MA.\n\n**Wedge ke examples:** chaaku, kulhadi (axe), chisel, keel (nail), darwaze ke neeche lagne wala **doorstop**, aur kheti mein **plough** (hal ka nukila hissa jo mitti ko cheerta hai).\n\n**Ek zaroori baat:** wedge bhi ek inclined plane hi hai (balki do). Bas fark ye hai ki dhalaan par load chadhane ke bajaye, yahan dhalaan khud cheez ke andar ghusti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Screw",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Screw",
            "body": "A **screw** is simply **an inclined plane wrapped helically around a central cylinder**.\n\nYou can prove this elegant relationship yourself: cut a right-angled triangle out of paper (an inclined plane) and wrap it tightly around a cylindrical pencil. The slanting hypotenuse forms a continuous, spiral ridge winding around the pencil. That spiral ridge is the **thread** of a screw!\n\n### Pitch of a Screw\n\nThe axial distance between two consecutive thread crests is known as the **pitch ($p$)**. When a screw is rotated through **one complete revolution ($360^\\circ$)**, it advances into the material by an axial distance exactly equal to its pitch:\n$$\\text{Axial Advance in 1 Revolution} = p$$\n\n```\n   ┌────────────────────────┐\n   │ ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱ │   ← Helical threads along shaft\n   └────────────────────────┘\n     ←p→\n     Distance between adjacent threads = Pitch p\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.3** – Pitch $p$: The linear advance made by a screw during one full rotation.\n\n### Mechanical Advantage of a Screw Jack\n\nIn a screw mechanism (such as an automotive screw jack or bench vice), the input effort $E$ acts at the end of a lever handle of length $r$, sweeping through the circumference of a circle ($2\\pi r$) during one revolution. Meanwhile, the heavy load $L$ advances by the small pitch distance $p$.\n\nBy the principle of work ($E \\times d_E = L \\times d_L$):\n$$E \\times (2\\pi r) = L \\times p$$\n\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{Ideal MA} = \\text{VR} = \\frac{\\text{Circumference of Effort Circle}}{\\text{Pitch of Screw}} = \\frac{2\\pi r}{p}}$$\n\n*(Throughout this topic, we use $\\pi \\approx 3.14$.)*\n\nBecause the handle radius $r$ is typically tens of centimeters while the pitch $p$ is only a few millimeters, the ratio $\\frac{2\\pi r}{p}$ is enormous—often ranging between $200$ and $800$! This explains why Mr. Sterling's screw jack in Topic 2 could lift an entire van with modest manual effort.\n\n**Examples of Screws:** Threaded machine bolts, jar lids, bottle caps, automotive screw jacks, carpenter's bench vices, helical drill bits, and Archimedes screws.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Screw** ek **cylinder par lipti hui inclined plane** hai. Agar tum ek tirchhe kate hue kagaz (triangle) ko pencil par lapeto, to uski slanting dhar pencil par ghumavdaar line bana degi: wahi **thread** hai.\n\n### Pitch\n\nDo paas-paas ke threads ke beech ki doori **pitch (p)** kehlati hai. Screw ko ek **poora chakkar** ghumao, to wo apni **pitch ke barabar** doori aage (ya peeche) badhta hai.\n\n```\n   ┌────────────────────────┐\n   │ ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱  ╱ │   ← threads (screw ka body)\n   └────────────────────────┘\n     ←p→\n     do paas-paas ke threads ke beech ki doori = pitch p\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.3** – Pitch p: ek poore chakkar mein screw itna hi aage badhta hai.\n\n### Screw ka MA\n\nScrew ka effort ek **circle** mein ghumta hai (jaise spanner ya handle ka sira), aur load pitch ke barabar upar uthta hai. **Screw jack** ka ideal MA (given result):\n\n> **MA = (effort ke circle ki circumference) ÷ pitch = 2π r ÷ p**\n\nYahan r = handle ki lambai, aur 2π r handle ke sire dwara banaye gaye circle ki circumference hai. Is topic mein hum π = 3.14 lenge.\n\nYahi **Topic 2 ki jack ki kahani** ka raaz hai: chhota effort ek bada circle banata hai, aur load bahut chhoti si pitch jitna upar uthta hai. Isliye MA bahut bada ho jaata hai.\n\n**Screw ke examples:** bolt, jar ka lid, bottle ka cap, screw jack, vice, drill bit."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Wheel-and-Axle",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Wheel-and-axle",
            "body": "A **wheel-and-axle** consists of two rigidly joined concentric circular cylinders of different diameters: a larger **wheel** of radius $R$ bonded to a smaller **axle** of radius $r$. The assembly rotates together on a common central axis.\n\nThe input **effort is applied tangentially to the rim of the large wheel**, while the **load is carried by a rope wound around the smaller axle**.\n\n```\n           Effort applied at wheel rim\n                 v\n           ╭───────────╮\n          ╱   ╭─────╮   ╲\n         │    │Axle │    │     R = Radius of large wheel\n         │    │  r  │    │     r = Radius of small axle\n          ╲   ╰─────╯   ╱\n           ╰───────────╯\n                 │\n               Load suspended from axle cord\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.4** – Wheel-and-axle: Effort acts on the outer circumference of radius $R$, while the load hangs from the axle of radius $r$.\n\nIn one complete rotation of the assembly:\n- The effort moves through a distance equal to the circumference of the wheel: $d_E = 2\\pi R$.\n- The load moves through a distance equal to the circumference of the axle: $d_L = 2\\pi r$.\n\nTherefore, the velocity ratio is:\n$$\\text{VR} = \\frac{d_E}{d_L} = \\frac{2\\pi R}{2\\pi r} = \\frac{R}{r}$$\n\nFor an ideal, frictionless system:\n\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{Ideal MA} = \\text{VR} = \\frac{\\text{Radius of Wheel (}R\\text{)}}{\\text{Radius of Axle (}r\\text{)}} = \\frac{R}{r}}$$\n\nThe larger the wheel relative to the axle, the greater the mechanical advantage.\n\n### Analogy to a Continuous Rotating Lever\n\nA wheel-and-axle can be conceptualized as a **continuous, 360-degree lever**: the central axis acts as the fulcrum, the radius of the wheel $R$ acts as the effort arm, and the radius of the axle $r$ acts as the load arm.\n\n**Everyday Examples of the Wheel-and-Axle:**\n- **Doorknobs:** Turning the broad outer knob rotates the narrow internal latch spindle easily.\n- **Automotive Steering Wheel:** A wide steering wheel allows drivers to turn heavy front wheels with modest arm force.\n- **Screwdrivers:** The thick plastic handle represents the wheel, and the thin metal shaft represents the axle (which is why a screwdriver multiplies rotational torque).\n- **Water Well Windlass:** A crank handle of radius $R$ winding a rope drum of radius $r$.\n- **Water Faucet Handles and Bicycle Pedals.**",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Wheel-and-axle** mein ek **bada wheel (radius R)** ek **chhote axle (radius r)** se jama hota hai, aur dono saath ghumte hain. **Effort wheel ke rim par** lagta hai, **load axle par**.\n\n```\n           effort (rim par)\n                v\n          ╭───────────╮\n         ╱   ╭─────╮   ╲\n        │    │axle │    │     R = wheel ka radius\n        │    │  r  │    │     r = axle ka radius\n         ╲   ╰─────╯   ╱\n          ╰───────────╯\n                │\n              load (axle ki rassi se)\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.4** – Bada wheel R, chhota axle r, effort rim par, load axle par.\n\nEk poore chakkar mein effort wheel ke rim par 2π R doori chalta hai, aur load axle ki 2π r doori chalta hai. To:\n\n> **ideal MA = VR = R ÷ r**\n\nJitna bada wheel aur jitna patla axle, utna bada MA.\n\n**Wheel-and-axle ke examples:** darwaze ka knob, steering wheel, screwdriver (handle bada, blade chhota; isliye Topic 1 mein ise force multiplier kaha tha), **windlass** (kuein ka handle), water-tap ka handle, aur cycle ka pedal aur wheel.\n\n### Lever se rishta\n\nWheel-and-axle ko **lagatar ghumta hua lever** samajh sakte ho: wheel ka radius effort ki arm hai aur axle ka radius load ki arm."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: The Four Simple Machines in Summary",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Chaaron machines ek table mein",
            "body": "| Machine | Governing Dimensions | Ideal MA ($\\text{VR}$) | Typical Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Inclined Plane** | Length along slope $l$, vertical height $h$ | $\\frac{l}{h}$ | Cargo ramp, wheelchair ramp, mountain road, stairs |\n| **Wedge** | Slanted side length $l$, head thickness $t$ | $\\frac{l}{t}$ | Woodchopping axe, chisel, knife blade, nail, plow |\n| **Screw (Jack)** | Handle length $r$, thread pitch $p$ | $\\frac{2\\pi r}{p}$ | Machine bolt, jar lid, screw jack, bench vice |\n| **Wheel-and-Axle** | Wheel radius $R$, axle radius $r$ | $\\frac{R}{r}$ | Doorknob, steering wheel, windlass, screwdriver |\n\nIn every ideal machine, $\\text{MA} = \\text{VR}$. In real physical machines, friction ensures $\\text{MA} < \\text{VR}$, with efficiency $\\eta = (\\text{MA} / \\text{VR}) \\times 100\\%$.",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Machine | Ideal MA | VR | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Inclined plane | l ÷ h | l ÷ h | Ramp, slide, hill road, stairs |\n| Wedge | l ÷ t | l ÷ t | Chaaku, kulhadi, chisel, keel, plough |\n| Screw (jack) | 2π r ÷ p | 2π r ÷ p | Bolt, jar lid, screw jack, vice |\n| Wheel-and-axle | R ÷ r | R ÷ r | Door knob, steering wheel, windlass |\n\nIdeal machine mein **MA = VR** hota hai. Asli machine mein friction ki wajah se **MA < VR**, aur efficiency η = (MA ÷ VR) × 100% hoti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Solved examples",
            "body": "**Example 1 (Inclined Plane Effort and MA):** A loading ramp has a length of $6\\text{ m}$ and reaches a storage deck $1.5\\text{ m}$ high. Calculate the ideal mechanical advantage and the effort needed to roll an $800\\text{-N}$ machine crate up the ramp.\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{6\\text{ m}}{1.5\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{4}$\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{800\\text{ N}}{4} = \\mathbf{200\\text{ N}}$\n\n*Check:* $\\text{Work Input} = 200\\text{ N} \\times 6\\text{ m} = 1200\\text{ J} = 800\\text{ N} \\times 1.5\\text{ m} = \\text{Work Output}$ ✅.\n\n**Example 2 (Determining Required Incline Length):** A worker must hoist a safe weighing $900\\text{ N}$ to an elevation of $1.5\\text{ m}$. If the worker can exert an effort of only $300\\text{ N}$, what minimum length of ramp is required under ideal conditions?\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{Effort}} = \\frac{900\\text{ N}}{300\\text{ N}} = 3$\n- $l = \\text{MA} \\times h = 3 \\times 1.5\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{4.5\\text{ m}}$\n\n*Check:* $300\\text{ N} \\times 4.5\\text{ m} = 1350\\text{ J} = 900\\text{ N} \\times 1.5\\text{ m}$ ✅.\n\n**Example 3 (Real Incline with Friction):** An inclined plane has $l = 5\\text{ m}$ and $h = 1\\text{ m}$. An effort of $125\\text{ N}$ is required to push a $500\\text{-N}$ barrel up the slope. Determine the actual $\\text{MA}$, the $\\text{VR}$, the efficiency, the ideal effort, and the energy lost to friction.\n\n- $\\text{Actual MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{Effort}} = \\frac{500\\text{ N}}{125\\text{ N}} = \\mathbf{4}$\n- $\\text{VR} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{5\\text{ m}}{1\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{5}$\n- $\\eta = \\frac{\\text{MA}}{\\text{VR}} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{80\\%}$\n- $\\text{Ideal Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{VR}} = \\frac{500\\text{ N}}{5} = \\mathbf{100\\text{ N}}$\n- $\\text{Work Input} = 125\\text{ N} \\times 5\\text{ m} = 625\\text{ J}$; $\\text{Work Output} = 500\\text{ N} \\times 1\\text{ m} = 500\\text{ J}$\n- $\\text{Energy Lost} = 625\\text{ J} - 500\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{125\\text{ J}}$ (dissipated as heat)\n\n*Check:* $\\frac{500\\text{ J}}{625\\text{ J}} = 0.80 = 80\\%$ ✅.\n\n**Example 4 (Mechanical Advantage of a Wedge):** A timber wedge has a slanting slope length of $10\\text{ cm}$ and a head thickness of $2\\text{ cm}$. Calculate its ideal mechanical advantage.\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{t} = \\frac{10\\text{ cm}}{2\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{5}$\n\n*Check:* Both lengths are in centimeters, giving a dimensionless ratio of 5 ✅.\n\n**Example 5 (Splitting Force of an Axe):** An axe blade has a slanting blade length of $12\\text{ cm}$ and a spine thickness of $3\\text{ cm}$. When struck with an effective driving effort of $50\\text{ N}$, what lateral outward splitting force does it exert on the log?\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{t} = \\frac{12\\text{ cm}}{3\\text{ cm}} = 4$\n- $\\text{Splitting Force (Load)} = \\text{MA} \\times \\text{Effort} = 4 \\times 50\\text{ N} = \\mathbf{200\\text{ N}}$\n\n*Check:* $\\frac{200\\text{ N}}{50\\text{ N}} = 4$ ✅.\n\n**Example 6 (Screw Jack MA):** An automotive screw jack features a crank handle of length $40\\text{ cm}$ and a screw thread pitch of $5\\text{ mm}$. Calculate its ideal mechanical advantage. (Take $\\pi = 3.14$).\n\n- Convert to consistent SI units: $r = 40\\text{ cm} = 0.4\\text{ m}$; $p = 5\\text{ mm} = 0.005\\text{ m}$.\n- Circumference $= 2\\pi r = 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 0.4\\text{ m} = 2.512\\text{ m}$.\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{2\\pi r}{p} = \\frac{2.512\\text{ m}}{0.005\\text{ m}} = 502.4 \\approx \\mathbf{502}$\n\n*Check:* $502.4 \\times 0.005 = 2.512\\text{ m}$ ✅.\n\n**Example 7 (Lifting Force of a Screw Jack):** Using the jack from Example 6 ($\\text{MA} = 502.4$), what ideal effort is needed to hoist a vehicle corner imposing a load of $10000\\text{ N}$?\n\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{10000\\text{ N}}{502.4} \\approx \\mathbf{19.9\\text{ N}}$ (approximately $20\\text{ N}$)\n\n*Check:* In one full turn, work input $= 19.9\\text{ N} \\times 2.512\\text{ m} \\approx 50\\text{ J}$, and work output $= 10000\\text{ N} \\times 0.005\\text{ m} = 50\\text{ J}$ ✅. A tiny effort of $20\\text{ N}$ lifts a one-ton mass.\n\n**Example 8 (Wheel-and-Axle Performance):** A wheel-and-axle assembly has a wheel radius of $30\\text{ cm}$ and an axle radius of $5\\text{ cm}$. What effort applied at the rim is required to raise a load of $600\\text{ N}$?\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{30\\text{ cm}}{5\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{6}$\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{600\\text{ N}}{6} = \\mathbf{100\\text{ N}}$\n\n*Check:* $100\\text{ N} \\times 6 = 600\\text{ N}$ ✅.\n\n**Example 9 (Well Windlass):** A water well windlass features a crank handle radius of $40\\text{ cm}$ and a rope drum radius of $10\\text{ cm}$. Find the effort needed to hoist a water bucket weighing $200\\text{ N}$.\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{40\\text{ cm}}{10\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{4}$\n- $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{200\\text{ N}}{4} = \\mathbf{50\\text{ N}}$\n\n*Check:* $50\\text{ N} \\times 4 = 200\\text{ N}$ ✅.\n\n**Example 10 (Comparing Wheelchair Ramps):** A building entrance requires a vertical rise of $0.3\\text{ m}$. The combined weight of a visitor and their wheelchair is $900\\text{ N}$. Ramp A has a length of $1.5\\text{ m}$; Ramp B has a length of $3.6\\text{ m}$. Calculate the ideal effort for both ramps.\n\n- **Ramp A:**\n  $$\\text{MA} = \\frac{1.5\\text{ m}}{0.3\\text{ m}} = 5 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\text{Effort} = \\frac{900\\text{ N}}{5} = \\mathbf{180\\text{ N}}$$\n- **Ramp B:**\n  $$\\text{MA} = \\frac{3.6\\text{ m}}{0.3\\text{ m}} = 12 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\text{Effort} = \\frac{900\\text{ N}}{12} = \\mathbf{75\\text{ N}}$$\n\n*Check:* Work done on A: $180\\text{ N} \\times 1.5\\text{ m} = 270\\text{ J}$. Work done on B: $75\\text{ N} \\times 3.6\\text{ m} = 270\\text{ J}$. Direct vertical lift: $900\\text{ N} \\times 0.3\\text{ m} = 270\\text{ J}$ ✅. Ramp B requires less than half the pushing force of Ramp A, making it vastly safer and more accessible.\n\n**Example 11 (Efficiency of a Real Wheel-and-Axle):** A wheel-and-axle with $R = 20\\text{ cm}$ and $r = 4\\text{ cm}$ lifts a load of $400\\text{ N}$ when driven by an effort of $100\\text{ N}$. Determine its $\\text{VR}$, $\\text{MA}$, and efficiency.\n\n- $\\text{VR} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{20\\text{ cm}}{4\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{5}$\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{400\\text{ N}}{100\\text{ N}} = \\mathbf{4}$\n- $\\eta = \\frac{\\text{MA}}{\\text{VR}} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{80\\%}$\n\n*Check:* If the load ascends $0.1\\text{ m}$, the effort travels $5 \\times 0.1\\text{ m} = 0.5\\text{ m}$. $\\text{Work Input} = 100\\text{ N} \\times 0.5\\text{ m} = 50\\text{ J}$; $\\text{Work Output} = 400\\text{ N} \\times 0.1\\text{ m} = 40\\text{ J}$. Efficiency $= 40 / 50 = 80\\%$ ✅.\n\n**Example 12 (Mountain Switchback Road):** A mountain road traverses a total distance of $2000\\text{ m}$ to overcome a vertical rise of $200\\text{ m}$. What ideal tractive force must a $20000\\text{-N}$ delivery vehicle generate to climb the pass?\n\n- $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{2000\\text{ m}}{200\\text{ m}} = 10$\n- $\\text{Force} = \\frac{20000\\text{ N}}{10} = \\mathbf{2000\\text{ N}}$\n\n*Check:* $\\text{Work} = 2000\\text{ N} \\times 2000\\text{ m} = 4,000,000\\text{ J} = 20000\\text{ N} \\times 200\\text{ m}$ ✅. Winding switchbacks keep the engine force within safe limits.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 1 (incline ka effort aur MA):** Ek ramp ki lambai 6 m aur unchai 1.5 m hai. 800 N ka load ideal case mein chadhana hai. MA aur effort?\n\n- MA = l ÷ h = 6 m ÷ 1.5 m = **4**\n- Effort = load ÷ MA = 800 N ÷ 4 = **200 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** effort × l = 200 N × 6 m = 1200 J, aur load × h = 800 N × 1.5 m = 1200 J ✅\n\n**Example 2 (kitni lambi dhalaan chahiye):** 900 N ka load 1.5 m ooncha uthana hai, aur effort sirf 300 N lagana hai (ideal). Dhalaan ki lambai?\n\n- MA = load ÷ effort = 900 N ÷ 300 N = 3\n- l = MA × h = 3 × 1.5 m = **4.5 m**\n\n**Jaanch:** effort × l = 300 N × 4.5 m = 1350 J, load × h = 900 N × 1.5 m = 1350 J ✅\n\n**Example 3 (work ideal aur real):** Ek incline l = 5 m, h = 1 m hai. 500 N ka load chadhane mein **125 N** ka effort laga. MA, VR, efficiency, ideal effort aur lost energy?\n\n- MA = load ÷ effort = 500 N ÷ 125 N = **4**\n- VR = l ÷ h = 5 m ÷ 1 m = **5**\n- η = (MA ÷ VR) × 100% = (4 ÷ 5) × 100% = **80%**\n- Ideal effort = load ÷ VR = 500 N ÷ 5 = **100 N**\n- Work input = 125 N × 5 m = 625 J, work output = 500 N × 1 m = 500 J\n- Lost energy = 625 J − 500 J = **125 J** (friction mein gaya)\n\n**Jaanch:** output ÷ input = 500 J ÷ 625 J = 0.8 = 80% ✅ (MA ÷ VR wale se milta hai).\n\n**Example 4 (wedge ka MA):** Ek wedge ki slope ki lambai 10 cm aur motai 2 cm hai. MA?\n\n- MA = l ÷ t = 10 cm ÷ 2 cm = **5**\n\n**Jaanch:** dono lambai ek hi unit (cm) mein hain, to ratio unit-free 5 aaya ✅\n\n**Example 5 (kulhadi):** Ek kulhadi ke blade ki slope 12 cm aur motai 3 cm hai. Lakdi ko cheerne ke liye 50 N ka effort lagta hai. Ideal load (side ka force)?\n\n- MA = 12 cm ÷ 3 cm = 4\n- Load = MA × effort = 4 × 50 N = **200 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** load ÷ effort = 200 N ÷ 50 N = 4 ✅\n\n**Example 6 (screw jack ka MA):** Ek screw jack ke handle ki lambai 40 cm hai aur pitch 5 mm. MA?\n\n- r = 40 cm = 0.4 m, p = 5 mm = 0.005 m (**pehle dono ko metre mein**)\n- Circumference = 2π r = 2 × 3.14 × 0.4 m = 2.512 m\n- MA = 2.512 m ÷ 0.005 m = 502.4 ≈ **502**\n\n**Jaanch:** 502.4 × 0.005 = 2.512 ✅\n\n**Example 7 (screw jack ka effort):** Example 6 wale jack se 10000 N ka load uthana hai. Ideal effort?\n\n- Effort = load ÷ MA = 10000 N ÷ 502.4 ≈ **19.9 N** (lagbhag 20 N)\n\n**Jaanch:** ek poore chakkar mein effort ka kaam = 19.9 N × 2.512 m ≈ 50 J, aur load ka kaam = 10000 N × 0.005 m = 50 J ✅. Isi wajah se sirf lagbhag 20 N ke effort se 10000 N (lagbhag 1000 kg) ka load uth jaata hai.\n\n**Example 8 (wheel-and-axle MA aur effort):** Wheel ka radius 30 cm, axle ka 5 cm. 600 N ka load. MA aur ideal effort?\n\n- MA = R ÷ r = 30 cm ÷ 5 cm = **6**\n- Effort = 600 N ÷ 6 = **100 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** 100 N × 6 = 600 N ✅\n\n**Example 9 (windlass):** Windlass ke handle ka radius 40 cm aur drum ka radius 10 cm hai. 200 N ki balti uthani hai. MA aur effort?\n\n- MA = R ÷ r = 40 cm ÷ 10 cm = **4**\n- Effort = 200 N ÷ 4 = **50 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** 50 N × 4 = 200 N ✅\n\n**Example 10 (wheelchair ke do ramps):** Ek raste mein 0.3 m ki unchai chadhni hai. Wheelchair aur vyakti ka total weight 900 N. Ramp A ki lambai 1.5 m, ramp B ki 3.6 m. Dono mein ideal effort?\n\n- Ramp A: MA = 1.5 m ÷ 0.3 m = 5, effort = 900 N ÷ 5 = **180 N**\n- Ramp B: MA = 3.6 m ÷ 0.3 m = 12, effort = 900 N ÷ 12 = **75 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** A: 180 N × 1.5 m = 270 J, B: 75 N × 3.6 m = 270 J, aur 900 N × 0.3 m = 270 J ✅. Ramp B mein zor kam, par lambai zyada. Wheelchair ke liye B behtar, bas jagah chahiye.\n\n**Example 11 (wheel-and-axle ki efficiency):** Ek wheel-and-axle mein R = 20 cm aur r = 4 cm hai. 400 N ka load uthane ke liye 100 N ka effort laga. VR, MA aur efficiency?\n\n- VR = R ÷ r = 20 cm ÷ 4 cm = **5**\n- MA = 400 N ÷ 100 N = **4**\n- η = (4 ÷ 5) × 100% = **80%**\n\n**Jaanch:** Agar load 0.1 m upar jaye, to effort 5 × 0.1 m = 0.5 m chalta hai. Work input = 100 N × 0.5 m = 50 J, output = 400 N × 0.1 m = 40 J, 40 ÷ 50 = 80% ✅\n\n**Example 12 (hill road):** Ek pahadi sadak 2000 m lambi hai aur 200 m ooncha chadhati hai. 20000 N ki gaadi ke liye ideal force?\n\n- MA = 2000 m ÷ 200 m = 10\n- Force = 20000 N ÷ 10 = **2000 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** 2000 N × 2000 m = 4000000 J, aur 20000 N × 200 m = 4000000 J ✅. Sadak lambi ghoom kar banayi jaati hai (hairpin bends), isi liye."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Believing a gentler ramp reduces total work**\n   As proven in Sophia's scenario, whether a ramp is $2\\text{ m}$ or $4\\text{ m}$ long, the ideal work required to elevate a given load through height $h$ remains strictly fixed ($1200\\text{ J}$). A gentler ramp reduces instantaneous effort, but increases travel distance proportionately.\n\n2. **Mistake 2: Inverting l and h in the inclined plane formula**\n   Remember: **$\\text{MA} = l / h$** ($\\text{slant length} \\div \\text{vertical height}$). Since the slanting hypotenuse $l$ is always longer than the vertical height $h$, the ideal $\\text{MA}$ of a ramp is always greater than 1. Dividing $1.5 \\div 6 = 0.25$ is an inversion error.\n\n3. **Mistake 3: Inverting R and r in the wheel-and-axle formula**\n   When effort is applied to the wheel and load is on the axle, **$\\text{MA} = R / r$** ($\\text{large radius} \\div \\text{small radius}$). If effort were applied to the axle to turn a large wheel (as in the rear wheel of a bicycle), $\\text{MA}$ would be less than 1, acting as a speed multiplier. Always identify where the effort is applied.\n\n4. **Mistake 4: Mixing millimeters, centimeters, and meters in screw calculations**\n   In Example 6, the handle radius was $40\\text{ cm}$ and pitch was $5\\text{ mm}$. If you compute $\\frac{2 \\times 3.14 \\times 40}{5}$, you get $50.24$, introducing a 10-fold error! Always convert all dimensions to standard meters ($r = 0.4\\text{ m}$, $p = 0.005\\text{ m}$) before dividing.\n\n5. **Mistake 5: Failing to recognize that a wedge is an inclined plane**\n   A wedge is not a separate physical invention; it is structurally two inclined planes joined back-to-back. The only difference is operational: an inclined plane stays fixed while the load moves, whereas a wedge moves while penetrating the load.\n\n6. **Mistake 6: Assuming screw MA depends solely on pitch**\n   The mechanical advantage of a screw depends equally on the **length of the turning handle** and the **thread pitch**: $\\text{MA} = 2\\pi r / p$. A longer crank handle increases $\\text{MA}$ just as effectively as a finer thread pitch.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Gentle ramp se kaam kam ho jata hai\"**\n\nNahi. Zeenat ke sawal ka jawab: plank 2 m ho ya 4 m, ideal work 1200 J hi hai. Force kam hota hai, par distance badh jaati hai.\n\n**Galti 2: l aur h ulat dena**\n\nMA = l ÷ h (slant length ÷ seedhi unchai). Agar ulta kar diya, to MA 1 se bhi kam aayega, jo ramp ke liye ajeeb lagta hai. Example 1 mein MA = 6 ÷ 1.5 = 4 hai, 1.5 ÷ 6 = 0.25 nahi.\n\n**Galti 3: Wheel-and-axle mein R aur r mila dena**\n\nMA = **R ÷ r** (bada ÷ chhota). Agar effort axle par aur load wheel par ho, to MA 1 se kam ho jaata hai. Hamesha dekho ki effort kahan lagta hai.\n\n**Galti 4: mm aur cm ko metre mein nahi badalna**\n\nExample 6 mein r = 0.4 m aur p = 0.005 m liya. Agar 40 cm aur 5 mm direct bhar dete, to 2 × 3.14 × 40 ÷ 5 = 50.24 aata, jo galat hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Wedge inclined plane nahi hai\"**\n\nWedge do inclined planes ka jod hi hai, peeth se peeth jodkar.\n\n**Galti 6: Screw ka MA sirf pitch se nikalna**\n\nScrew ka MA **handle ki lambai aur pitch, dono** par depend karta hai: MA = 2π r ÷ p. Handle jitna lamba, ya pitch jitni chhoti, MA utna bada."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Inclined Plane:** A sloped flat surface; ideal $\\text{MA} = \\text{VR} = l / h$. A longer ramp reduces the required effort, but demands a longer travel path.\n- Work is conserved under ideal conditions: $\\text{Work Input} = E \\times l = L \\times h = \\text{Work Output}$.\n- Real ramps experience friction, resulting in $\\eta < 100\\%$ and energy dissipation as heat.\n- **Wedge:** Two inclined planes joined back-to-back; ideal $\\text{MA} = l / t$ (axes, chisels, knife edges, nails).\n- **Screw:** An inclined plane wrapped helically around a cylinder. Pitch ($p$) is the linear advance per revolution. For a screw jack, $\\text{Ideal MA} = \\text{VR} = 2\\pi r / p$.\n- **Wheel-and-Axle:** Concentric cylinders rotating together; ideal $\\text{MA} = \\text{VR} = R / r$. Can be viewed as a continuous rotating lever.\n- Always convert all linear measurements to consistent units (preferably meters) before evaluating ratios.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Inclined plane:** tirchhi surface; ideal MA = VR = l ÷ h. Lambi dhalaan se effort kam, rasta zyada.\n- Work input = effort × l = load × h = work output (ideal). **Kaam wahi**, force aur doori ka trade-off.\n- Real incline mein friction se η < 100%. Example 3: η = 80%, lost energy = 125 J.\n- **Wedge:** do inclined planes peeth se peeth; ideal MA = l ÷ t (chaaku, kulhadi, keel, plough).\n- **Screw:** cylinder par lipti hui inclined plane; pitch = ek chakkar mein aage badhne ki doori; screw jack ka MA = 2π r ÷ p.\n- **Wheel-and-axle:** ideal MA = VR = R ÷ r; lagatar ghumta hua lever.\n- Hamesha cm aur mm ko m mein badal kar hi MA nikalo."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. A warehouse ramp is $8\\text{ m}$ long and reaches a platform elevated $2\\text{ m}$ above the ground. Calculate the ideal mechanical advantage and the effort needed to push an $800\\text{-N}$ packing crate up the ramp.\n\n2. A heavy machine weighing $600\\text{ N}$ must be elevated through a vertical height of $1.2\\text{ m}$ using an ideal ramp. If the available pushing effort is limited to $150\\text{ N}$, what minimum length of ramp is required?\n\n3. An inclined plane has a length of $l = 6\\text{ m}$ and a height of $h = 1.5\\text{ m}$. A load of $840\\text{ N}$ is pushed up the slope by an effort of $280\\text{ N}$. Calculate the actual mechanical advantage, velocity ratio, operational efficiency, and the energy dissipated by friction.\n\n4. A splitting wedge has a slanting slope length of $15\\text{ cm}$ and a head thickness of $3\\text{ cm}$. Find its ideal mechanical advantage. What lateral splitting force does it exert on a log when struck with an effort of $80\\text{ N}$?\n\n5. An automotive screw jack has a crank handle of length $0.5\\text{ m}$ and a thread pitch of $4\\text{ mm}$. Calculate its ideal mechanical advantage, and determine the effort needed to lift a vehicle corner imposing a load of $7850\\text{ N}$. (Take $\\pi = 3.14$).\n\n6. A wheel-and-axle hoist has a wheel radius of $50\\text{ cm}$ and an axle radius of $10\\text{ cm}$. Determine its ideal mechanical advantage and the effort required to elevate a load of $750\\text{ N}$.\n\n7. A water well windlass features a crank handle radius of $60\\text{ cm}$ and a rope drum radius of $15\\text{ cm}$. Determine the effort required to hoist a water bucket weighing $240\\text{ N}$ under ideal conditions.\n\n8. State whether each of the following statements is True or False:\n   (a) A gentler incline reduces the total mechanical work needed to elevate a load to a given height.\n   (b) A wedge is structurally equivalent to two inclined planes joined back-to-back.\n   (c) A screw with a smaller pitch has a larger mechanical advantage, assuming handle length is kept constant.\n   (d) For a wheel-and-axle with effort on the wheel, the ideal mechanical advantage is $r / R$.\n\n9. A wheel-and-axle assembly with $R = 25\\text{ cm}$ and $r = 5\\text{ cm}$ lifts a $300\\text{-N}$ load when driven by an effort of $75\\text{ N}$. Find its velocity ratio, mechanical advantage, and efficiency.\n\n10. Explain why mountain highways incorporate serpentine hairpin switchbacks rather than climbing straight up a mountain ridge. Frame your explanation in terms of force and travel distance.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{8\\text{ m}}{2\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{4}$.\n   $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{800\\text{ N}}{4} = \\mathbf{200\\text{ N}}$.\n   *Check:* $200\\text{ N} \\times 8\\text{ m} = 1600\\text{ J} = 800\\text{ N} \\times 2\\text{ m}$ ✅.\n\n2. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{Effort}} = \\frac{600\\text{ N}}{150\\text{ N}} = 4$.\n   $l = \\text{MA} \\times h = 4 \\times 1.2\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{4.8\\text{ m}}$.\n   *Check:* $150\\text{ N} \\times 4.8\\text{ m} = 720\\text{ J} = 600\\text{ N} \\times 1.2\\text{ m}$ ✅.\n\n3. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{840\\text{ N}}{280\\text{ N}} = \\mathbf{3}$.\n   $\\text{VR} = \\frac{l}{h} = \\frac{6\\text{ m}}{1.5\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{4}$.\n   $\\eta = \\frac{\\text{MA}}{\\text{VR}} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{75\\%}$.\n   $\\text{Work Input} = 280\\text{ N} \\times 6\\text{ m} = 1680\\text{ J}$.\n   $\\text{Work Output} = 840\\text{ N} \\times 1.5\\text{ m} = 1260\\text{ J}$.\n   $\\text{Energy Dissipated} = 1680\\text{ J} - 1260\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{420\\text{ J}}$.\n   *Check:* $\\frac{1260}{1680} = 0.75 = 75\\%$ ✅.\n\n4. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{l}{t} = \\frac{15\\text{ cm}}{3\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{5}$.\n   $\\text{Splitting Force} = \\text{MA} \\times \\text{Effort} = 5 \\times 80\\text{ N} = \\mathbf{400\\text{ N}}$.\n   *Check:* $\\frac{400}{80} = 5$ ✅.\n\n5. Effort circumference $= 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 0.5\\text{ m} = 3.14\\text{ m}$.\n   Pitch $p = 4\\text{ mm} = 0.004\\text{ m}$.\n   $\\text{MA} = \\frac{3.14\\text{ m}}{0.004\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{785}$.\n   $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{7850\\text{ N}}{785} = \\mathbf{10\\text{ N}}$.\n   *Check:* $10\\text{ N} \\times 785 = 7850\\text{ N}$ ✅.\n\n6. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{50\\text{ cm}}{10\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{5}$.\n   $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{750\\text{ N}}{5} = \\mathbf{150\\text{ N}}$.\n   *Check:* $150\\text{ N} \\times 5 = 750\\text{ N}$ ✅.\n\n7. $\\text{MA} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{60\\text{ cm}}{15\\text{ cm}} = 4$.\n   $\\text{Effort} = \\frac{240\\text{ N}}{4} = \\mathbf{60\\text{ N}}$.\n   *Check:* $60\\text{ N} \\times 4 = 240\\text{ N}$ ✅.\n\n8. (a) **False** ❌. A gentler slope reduces the required pushing effort, but the mechanical work required (under ideal conditions) remains identical because the distance increases proportionately.\n   (b) **True** ✅. A wedge is geometrically formed by two inclined planes placed base-to-base.\n   (c) **True** ✅. Because $\\text{MA} = 2\\pi r / p$, a smaller pitch $p$ increases the mechanical advantage.\n   (d) **False** ❌. When effort is applied at the wheel, ideal $\\text{MA} = R / r$.\n\n9. $\\text{VR} = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{25\\text{ cm}}{5\\text{ cm}} = \\mathbf{5}$.\n   $\\text{MA} = \\frac{300\\text{ N}}{75\\text{ N}} = \\mathbf{4}$.\n   $\\eta = \\frac{\\text{MA}}{\\text{VR}} \\times 100\\% = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{80\\%}$.\n   *Check:* Lifting load $0.1\\text{ m}$ requires effort to move $0.5\\text{ m}$: $\\text{Work Input} = 75 \\times 0.5 = 37.5\\text{ J}$; $\\text{Work Output} = 300 \\times 0.1 = 30\\text{ J}$; $\\frac{30}{37.5} = 80\\%$ ✅.\n\n10. A direct ascent up a mountainside represents a steep inclined plane with a very small length $l$, requiring an enormous driving force that vehicle engines cannot deliver. Serpentine switchbacks dramatically increase the road length $l$ for the same vertical rise $h$. This large ratio of $l / h$ significantly reduces the required driving force, allowing vehicles to climb steadily at the cost of traveling a longer distance.\n\n---\n\nSophia now clearly understands: a longer ramp makes rolling heavy drums manageable by reducing the required muscular force, but it does not reduce the total energy demanded by nature.\n\n**What's next?** How do all these simple machines unite under a single universal scientific law? In Topic 6, we examine the **Principle of Machines**, explore combinations of machines (compound machines such as bicycles), and provide a complete chapter wrap-up.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. A ramp is 8 m long and 2 m high. Find the ideal MA and the effort needed to push an 800 N load up it.\n\n2. A 600 N load must be raised through 1.2 m using an ideal incline with an effort of only 150 N. What length of incline is needed?\n\n3. An incline has l = 6 m and h = 1.5 m. A load of 840 N is raised using an effort of 280 N. Find the MA, VR, efficiency and the energy lost.\n\n4. A wedge has a slope length of 15 cm and a thickness of 3 cm. Find its ideal MA. What load does it exert on the wood if the effort is 80 N?\n\n5. A screw jack has a handle of length 0.5 m and a pitch of 4 mm. Find its ideal MA, and the effort needed to lift a load of 7850 N. Take π = 3.14.\n\n6. A wheel-and-axle has R = 50 cm and r = 10 cm. Find its ideal MA, and the effort needed for a 750 N load.\n\n7. A windlass has a handle radius of 60 cm and a drum radius of 15 cm. Find the effort needed to lift a 240 N bucket (ideal).\n\n8. State True or False:\n   (a) A gentler ramp reduces the work needed to lift a load.\n   (b) A wedge is made of two inclined planes placed back to back.\n   (c) A screw with a smaller pitch has a larger MA, if the handle length is the same.\n   (d) For a wheel-and-axle, ideal MA = r ÷ R.\n\n9. A wheel-and-axle with R = 25 cm and r = 5 cm lifts a 300 N load with an effort of 75 N. Find its VR, MA and efficiency.\n\n10. Why do mountain roads take hairpin bends instead of going straight up the slope? Answer in terms of force and distance.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. MA = l ÷ h = 8 m ÷ 2 m = **4**. Effort = 800 N ÷ 4 = **200 N**.\n   Jaanch: 200 × 8 = 1600 J, 800 × 2 = 1600 J ✅\n\n2. MA = 600 N ÷ 150 N = 4. l = MA × h = 4 × 1.2 m = **4.8 m**.\n   Jaanch: 150 × 4.8 = 720 J, 600 × 1.2 = 720 J ✅\n\n3. MA = 840 N ÷ 280 N = **3**. VR = 6 m ÷ 1.5 m = **4**. η = (3 ÷ 4) × 100% = **75%**. Work input = 280 N × 6 m = 1680 J, output = 840 N × 1.5 m = 1260 J, energy lost = 1680 J − 1260 J = **420 J**.\n   Jaanch: 1260 J ÷ 1680 J = 0.75 = 75% ✅\n\n4. MA = 15 cm ÷ 3 cm = **5**. Load = 5 × 80 N = **400 N**.\n   Jaanch: 400 ÷ 80 = 5 ✅\n\n5. Circumference = 2 × 3.14 × 0.5 m = 3.14 m. p = 4 mm = 0.004 m. MA = 3.14 ÷ 0.004 = **785**. Effort = 7850 N ÷ 785 = **10 N**.\n   Jaanch: 10 × 785 = 7850 ✅\n\n6. MA = 50 cm ÷ 10 cm = **5**. Effort = 750 N ÷ 5 = **150 N**.\n   Jaanch: 150 × 5 = 750 ✅\n\n7. MA = 60 cm ÷ 15 cm = 4. Effort = 240 N ÷ 4 = **60 N**.\n   Jaanch: 60 × 4 = 240 ✅\n\n8. (a) **False** ❌. Gentler ramp se effort kam hota hai, par work (ideal) wahi rehta hai.\n   (b) **True** ✅. Wedge do inclined planes peeth se peeth jodkar banta hai.\n   (c) **True** ✅. MA = 2π r ÷ p, p chhota to MA bada.\n   (d) **False** ❌. Ideal MA = R ÷ r.\n\n9. VR = 25 cm ÷ 5 cm = **5**. MA = 300 N ÷ 75 N = **4**. η = (4 ÷ 5) × 100% = **80%**.\n   Jaanch: Load 0.1 m uthe to effort 0.5 m chalta hai: input = 75 × 0.5 = 37.5 J, output = 300 × 0.1 = 30 J, 30 ÷ 37.5 = 80% ✅\n\n10. Seedhi chadhai mein slope bahut steep hoti hai aur gaadi ko bahut zyada force chahiye. Hairpin bends se sadak lambi ho jaati hai, to slope halki ho jaati hai aur force kam lagta hai. Ideal mein work wahi rehta hai: force kam, distance zyada.\n\n---\n\nAb Zeenat ko jawab mil gaya: lambi plank se zor kam lagta hai, par kaam nahi bachta, kyunki force ke saath distance badh jaati hai. Agli baar ramp, bolt ya darwaze ka handle dekho to sochna: ye kaun si machine hai, aur isme force aur distance ka kya sauda hua?\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab sab machines ko ek hi niyam se jodna: **machine kaam banati nahi, sirf badalti hai**. Ye hum Topic 6 mein dekhenge, aur poore chapter ka wrap-up bhi karenge."
          }
