          {
            "heading": "Part D: Comprehensive Comparison: Concave vs. Convex Mirrors",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Concave aur convex ki tulna",
            "body": "| Feature | Concave Mirror | Convex Mirror |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Physical profile** | Reflecting surface curves inward | Reflecting surface bulges outward |\n| **Reflective boundary** | Inner hollow spherical surface | Outer spherical surface |\n| **Optical action** | **Converging** (brings parallel rays together) | **Diverging** (spreads parallel rays apart) |\n| **Focus type** | **Real focus** (rays physically intersect) | **Virtual focus** (rays appear to diverge from point) |\n| **Location of $F$ and $C$** | **In front** of the reflecting surface | **Behind** the reflecting surface |\n| **Field of view** | Relatively narrow | **Extremely wide** (compresses a large panorama) |\n| **Everyday examples** | Spoon bowl, makeup mirror, headlight reflector | Spoon back, car side mirror, security corner dome |\n\nThe wide field of view of a convex mirror is invaluable: because outgoing rays diverge, light from a vast angular landscape is captured and directed into the driver's eye, displaying a broad panorama in a compact pane.",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Baat | Concave mirror | Convex mirror |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aakaar (shape) | Andar ki taraf dabi hui | Bahar ki taraf ubhri hui |\n| Reflecting side | Gole ke andar ki side (centre ki taraf) | Gole ki bahar ki side (centre se door) |\n| Doosra naam | Converging mirror | Diverging mirror |\n| Parallel kirnein reflect hone ke baad | Ek point F par ikatthi hoti hain | Phail jaati hain |\n| Focus ka type | Real focus (mirror ke aage) | Virtual focus (mirror ke peeche) |\n| C aur F kahan hain | Mirror ke aage | Mirror ke peeche |\n| Field of view (kitna area dikhta hai) | Kam | **Zyada**: convex mirror se bada area ek saath dikhta hai |\n| Example | Chammach ka bowl, katori ka andar | Chammach ki peeth, steel ki gend ki bahar ki surface |\n\nConvex mirror ka wide field of view hone ka matlab: usme peeche ka bahut bada area ek saath chhote roop mein dikh jaata hai. (Iska istemaal Topic 6 mein.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: New Cartesian Sign Convention (Class 10 Extension)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Thoda extra (Class 10 overlap): New Cartesian sign convention",
            "body": "To apply algebraic formulas rigorously to mirrors, physicists adopt the **New Cartesian Sign Convention**:\n\n1. The **pole ($P$)** is taken as the optical coordinate **origin $(0,0)$**.\n2. The **principal axis** serves as the horizontal **$x$-axis**.\n3. Light is assumed to travel from **left to right**.\n4. Distances measured in the **direction of incident light (to the right of $P$) are POSITIVE (+)**.\n5. Distances measured **against the direction of incident light (to the left of $P$) are NEGATIVE (−)**.\n6. Vertical heights measured **above the principal axis are POSITIVE (+)**; heights **below the axis are NEGATIVE (−)**.\n\n| Mirror Type | Sign of $R$ | Sign of $f$ | Position of $F$ and $C$ |\n|---|:---:|:---:|---|\n| **Concave Mirror** | **Negative (−)** | **Negative (−)** | In front of mirror (left of $P$) |\n| **Convex Mirror** | **Positive (+)** | **Positive (+)** | Behind mirror (right of $P$) |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The relationship $f = R/2$ holds with algebraic signs:\n> For concave: $f = (-R)/2 = -f$. For convex: $f = (+R)/2 = +f$.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mirror ke numbers (f, R, doori) likhte waqt alag-alag direction mein jaane par +/− lagana padta hai, taaki formula sab ke liye ek jaisa chale. Isko **sign convention** kehte hain. Yahan sirf shuruaat:\n\n1. **Pole P ko origin** (shunya, 0) maanenge.\n2. **Incident light baayen se daayen** chalti hai, aur principal axis ko x-axis maanenge.\n3. **Light ki direction mein naapi gayi doori positive (+)**, **light ki ulti direction mein naapi gayi doori negative (−).**\n\nConcave mirror ka F aur C mirror ke **aage** hain, yaani us taraf jahan se light aati hai. Light ki **ulti** direction mein naapte hain, isliye ye **negative** hain. Convex mirror ka F aur C mirror ke **peeche** hain, yaani light ki **same** direction mein, isliye ye **positive** hain.\n\n| Mirror | R ka sign | f ka sign | F aur C kahan hain |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Concave | Negative (−) | Negative (−) | Mirror ke aage |\n| Convex | Positive (+) | Positive (+) | Mirror ke peeche |\n\nYaani **concave mirror ki f aur R dono negative, convex mirror ki dono positive.** f = R ÷ 2 ka rishta sign ke saath bhi sahi rehta hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Activity: Estimating the Focal Length of a Concave Mirror",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Activity: concave mirror ki focal length naapna",
            "body": "Because the Sun is at an immense astronomical distance, sunlight rays reaching Earth are effectively parallel to each other. When directed along the principal axis of a concave mirror, these rays converge directly at the principal focus $F$.\n\n**Apparatus:** A concave mirror, a mirror stand, a white cardboard screen, and a metre ruler.\n\n**Procedure:**\n1. Mount the concave mirror on a bench facing an open outdoor window illuminated by direct sunlight.\n2. Hold the cardboard screen in front of the mirror. Slowly move the screen back and forth along the axis until you capture a **sharp, pinpoint, intensely bright spot of light** on the screen.\n3. Using the metre ruler, measure the straight-line distance between the pole $P$ of the mirror and the bright spot on the screen.\n\n```\n                                          )\n  ──────────►───────────────────────────────╱\n                                      │   ↙   )\n                                      │ ╱      )\n──────────────────────────────────────*─────────)─────────────────\n                                      │ ╲      )\n                                      │   ↖   )\n  ──────────►───────────────────────────────╲\n                                          )\n                                Card screen at F\n\n                                      ◄─ f = 15 cm ─►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.11** – Measuring focal length via solar projection. Parallel sunlight converges onto the card at focus $F$. The measured mirror-to-screen distance represents the focal length $f$.\n\n**Sample Measurement:**\n- Measured distance from mirror to focused sunspot: $d = 15.0\text{ cm}$.\n- Focal length: $f \u0007pprox \\mathbf{15.0\text{ cm}}$.\n- Estimated radius of curvature: $R = 2f = 2 \times 15.0\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{30.0\text{ cm}}$.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never look directly at the Sun or at its concentrated reflection in a concave mirror. The intense thermal concentration at $F$ can cause immediate, irreversible retinal burns. Do not hold the paper screen at the focus point for extended periods, as concentrated solar energy will ignite the paper.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Hum Sooraj ko bahut door ki cheez maan sakte hain, to uski kirnein lagbhag parallel hoti hain. Is se concave mirror ki focal length ka andaza lag sakta hai.\n\n**Zaroorat:** ek concave mirror (ya steel ki bahut chamakti katori ka andar ka hissa), ek screen (gatta ya safed kagaz), ek meter-scale.\n\n**Tarika:**\n1. Mirror ko haath mein ya stand par pakdo aur Sooraj (ya kisi bahut door ki roshan cheez, jaise dhoop mein chamakti window) ki taraf rakho.\n2. Mirror ke saamne screen (gatta) ko aage-peeche karo, jab tak screen par ek **bahut chhota, sabse chamakdaar spot** (sharp bright spot) na dikhne lage.\n3. Mirror (pole) aur screen ke beech ki doori meter-scale se naapo.\n\n```\n                                          )\n  ──────────►───────────────────────────────╱\n                                      │   ↙   )\n                                      │ ╱      )\n──────────────────────────────────────*─────────)─────────────────\n                                      │ ╲      )\n                                      │   ↖   )\n  ──────────►───────────────────────────────╲\n                                          )\n                                screen (card) F par\n\n                                      ◄─ f=15 ──►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.11** – Sooraj ki parallel kirnein concave mirror se reflect hokar screen par F par ek chhote chamakdaar spot mein ikatthi hoti hain. Mirror se spot tak ki doori lagbhag f hai.\n\n**Sample reading:** mirror se spot ki doori = 15 cm.\n- f ≈ **15 cm**\n- R = 2 × f = 2 × 15 cm = **30 cm**\n\nJaanch: f = R ÷ 2 = 30 cm ÷ 2 = 15 cm ✅\n\n**Savdhani:** Sooraj ki taraf seedha mat dekho, aur mirror se reflect hui dhoop kisi ki aankhon par mat daalo. Sirf screen par bane spot ko dekho. Spot bahut garm hota hai, isliye usse kagaz ya kapde ko zyada der mat tapne do.\n\n(Ye doori sirf **lagbhag** f hai, kyunki Sooraj bahut door hai par infinite door nahi, aur mirror ka aperture bhi chhota-bada hota hai.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part G: Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part G: Solved examples",
            "body": "In all examples below, mirrors possess small apertures where $f = R/2$.\n\n### Example 1: Finding $f$ from Radius of Curvature $R$\nA concave mirror has a radius of curvature $R = 30\text{ cm}$. Calculate its focal length.\n\n**Solution:**\n$$f = \frac{R}{2} = \frac{30\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{15\text{ cm}}$$\n*Verification:* $R = 2f = 2 \times 15\text{ cm} = 30\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n### Example 2: Finding $R$ from Focal Length $f$\nA convex mirror used in an automobile has a focal length of $12\text{ cm}$. Determine its radius of curvature.\n\n**Solution:**\n$$R = 2f = 2 \times 12\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{24\text{ cm}}$$\n*Verification:* $f = R/2 = 24/2 = 12\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n### Example 3: Spatial Positions of $C$ and $F$\nA concave mirror has $R = 36\text{ cm}$. Find the distance of focus $F$ and centre of curvature $C$ from the pole, and calculate the distance between $F$ and $C$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Distance of $F$ from pole:\n   $$f = PF = \frac{R}{2} = \frac{36\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{18\text{ cm}}\text{ (in front of the mirror)}$$\n2. Distance of $C$ from pole:\n   $$PC = R = \\mathbf{36\text{ cm}}\text{ (in front of the mirror)}$$\n3. Distance between $F$ and $C$:\n   $$FC = PC - PF = 36\text{ cm} - 18\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{18\text{ cm}} = f$$\n\n```\n C                F               P\n +────────────────+───────────────)\n ◄──── 18 cm ────►◄──── 18 cm ───►\n ◄───────────── 36 cm ───────────►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.12** – Spatial distribution on the principal axis. $PF = FC = 18\text{ cm}$, and total radius $PC = 36\text{ cm}$.\n\nFor a convex mirror with $R = 24\text{ cm}$: $f = 12\text{ cm}$; both $F$ and $C$ lie $12\text{ cm}$ and $24\text{ cm}$ **behind the mirror**, with $FC = 12\text{ cm}$. $\\checkmark$\n\n### Example 4: Identifying Mirror Type from Ray Behaviour\n1. Parallel rays strike Mirror A and converge to a point $8\text{ cm}$ in front of the mirror surface. Identify the mirror type and determine its $f$ and $R$.\n2. Parallel rays strike Mirror B and diverge such that they appear to emanate from a point $6\text{ cm}$ behind the mirror. Identify the mirror type and determine its $f$ and $R$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Rays converge to a real focus in front: **Concave mirror**.\n   $$f = \\mathbf{8\text{ cm}}, \\quad R = 2 \times 8\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{16\text{ cm}}$$\n2. Rays diverge from a virtual focus behind: **Convex mirror**.\n   $$f = \\mathbf{6\text{ cm}}, \\quad R = 2 \times 6\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{12\text{ cm}}$$\n*Verification:* $16/2 = 8\text{ cm}$ and $12/2 = 6\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n### Example 5: Focal Length from Solar Projection\nIn a physics lab, a student projects the image of the Sun onto a white screen using a concave mirror. The sharpest sunspot forms when the screen is $12.0\text{ cm}$ from the mirror pole. Determine $f$ and $R$.\n\n**Solution:**\nBecause sunlight arrives as parallel rays, the image forms at the principal focus:\n$$f \u0007pprox \\mathbf{12.0\text{ cm}}$$\n$$R = 2f = 2 \times 12.0\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{24.0\text{ cm}}$$\n*Verification:* $24/2 = 12\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n### Example 6: Applying Cartesian Signs to $R$ and $f$\n1. Express $R$ and $f$ with Cartesian signs for a concave mirror with a radius of $20\text{ cm}$.\n2. Express $R$ and $f$ with Cartesian signs for a convex mirror with a radius of $40\text{ cm}$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Concave mirror: Landmarks lie to the left of the pole (against incident light):\n   $$R = \\mathbf{-20\text{ cm}}, \\quad f = \frac{-20\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{-10\text{ cm}}$$\n2. Convex mirror: Landmarks lie to the right of the pole (in direction of incident light):\n   $$R = \\mathbf{+40\text{ cm}}, \\quad f = \frac{+40\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{+20\text{ cm}}$$\n*Verification:* $2 \times (-10) = -20\text{ cm}$ and $2 \times (+20) = +40\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n### Example 7: Identifying Aperture and Pole\nIn the diagram below, $A$ and $B$ represent the circular rim boundaries of a concave mirror. Point $M$ is the surface midpoint, and point $Q$ is located on the principal axis $20\text{ cm}$ in front of $M$, acting as the centre of curvature. The straight chord distance $AB = 4\text{ cm}$.\nIdentify: (a) the pole, (b) the aperture, (c) $R$ and $f$.\n\n```\n                                 A      ▲\n                                  )     │\n                                   )    │  AB = 4 cm\n Q ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ M)   ┼\n                                   )    │\n                                  )     │\n                                 B      ▼\n ◄─────────── MQ = 20 cm ──────────►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.13** – Measuring aperture and curvature. Midpoint $M$ is the pole, rim chord $AB$ is the aperture, and distance $MQ$ is the radius of curvature.\n\n**Solution:**\n- (a) The surface midpoint is the **pole**: $\\mathbf{P = M}$.\n- (b) The aperture is the diameter chord: $\text{Aperture} = AB = \\mathbf{4\text{ cm}}$.\n- (c) Radius $R = MQ = \\mathbf{20\text{ cm}}$, and $f = R/2 = 20/2 = \\mathbf{10\text{ cm}}$.\n*Verification:* Aperture ratio $= 4\text{ cm} / 20\text{ cm} = 0.20 \\ll 1$, confirming small-aperture paraxial validity. $\\checkmark$\n\n### Example 8: Tracing Three Standard Rays\nA concave mirror has a focal length $f = 10\text{ cm}$.\n1. Describe the trajectory of a ray parallel to the principal axis, traveling $2\text{ cm}$ above it.\n2. Describe the trajectory of a ray passing through the centre of curvature $C$. Where is $C$ located?\n3. Describe the trajectory of a ray passing through the focus $F$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. The parallel ray reflects to pass through focus $F$, intersecting the principal axis at $d = \\mathbf{10\text{ cm}}$ in front of the pole.\n2. $C$ is located at $R = 2f = 2 \times 10\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{20\text{ cm}}$ in front of the pole. A ray passing through $C$ strikes normally and **rebounds along its own path**.\n3. A ray passing through $F$ emerges **parallel to the principal axis** after reflection.\n*(If this same ray struck a convex mirror of $f = 10\text{ cm}$, it would diverge as though originating from $F$, $10\text{ cm}$ behind the glass).* $\\checkmark$\n\n### Example 9: Classifying 10 Everyday Surfaces\nClassify each item as a concave mirror, convex mirror, or plane mirror:\n\n| Everyday Object | Optical Classification | Physical Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Inner bowl of a soup spoon | **Concave** | Hollow surface curving inward toward centre |\n| 2. Outer back of a soup spoon | **Convex** | Rounded surface bulging outward |\n| 3. Automotive headlight reflector | **Concave** | Deeply curved bowl designed to collimate light |\n| 4. Vehicle wing side mirror | **Convex** | Bulging surface providing wide-angle field of view |\n| 5. Dentist's shaving/makeup mirror | **Concave** | Curved inward to produce magnified virtual view |\n| 6. Convenience store security mirror | **Convex** | Wide-angle dome viewing entire aisles |\n| 7. Flat stainless steel baking sheet | **Plane** | Planar, uncurved reflecting boundary |\n| 8. Chrome silver holiday ornament | **Convex** | Exterior spherical surface bulging in all directions |\n| 9. Soup ladle | **Concave (inner) / Convex (outer)** | Bowl acts as concave; exterior acts as convex |\n| 10. Dental examination probe mirror | **Concave** | Precision miniature concave mirror |\n\n### Example 10: Using $PF = FC = f$\n1. In a concave mirror, the linear distance from pole $P$ to centre of curvature $C$ is $50\text{ cm}$. Find $f$ and distance $FC$.\n2. In another curved mirror, distance $PF = 9\text{ cm}$. Find distance $FC$ and total radius $PC$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $PC = R = 50\text{ cm} \\implies f = PF = 50/2 = \\mathbf{25\text{ cm}}$, and $FC = PC - PF = 50 - 25 = \\mathbf{25\text{ cm}}$.\n2. $FC = PF = \\mathbf{9\text{ cm}}$, and $PC = PF + FC = 9 + 9 = \\mathbf{18\text{ cm}}$.\n*Verification:* $PF = FC = f$ holds in both cases. $\\checkmark$\n\n### Example 11: Unit Conversion and Ray 4 Geometry\n1. A concave mirror has a radius of curvature $R = 0.4\text{ m}$. Express its focal length in centimetres.\n2. A light ray strikes the pole $P$ of this concave mirror at an angle of $30^\\circ$ to the principal axis. Find the angle made by the reflected ray with the axis, and the total angle between the incident and reflected rays.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $f = R/2 = 0.4\text{ m} / 2 = 0.2\text{ m} = 0.2 \times 100\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{20\text{ cm}}$.\n2. At the pole, the principal axis is the normal. Therefore, $\u0007ngle i = 30^\\circ$.\n   By the First Law of Reflection: $\u0007ngle r = \\mathbf{30^\\circ}$ on the opposite side of the axis.\n   Total angle between rays: $\theta = \u0007ngle i + \u0007ngle r = 30^\\circ + 30^\\circ = \\mathbf{60^\\circ}$.\n*Verification:* $20\text{ cm} \times 2 = 40\text{ cm} = 0.4\text{ m} = R \\; \\checkmark$",
            "bodyHinglish": "Is poore Part mein mirror chhote aperture wala hai aur f = R ÷ 2 lagega.\n\n### Example 1: R se f nikaalo\n\nEk concave mirror ki radius of curvature R = 30 cm hai. Focal length kya hai?\n\n- f = R ÷ 2 = 30 cm ÷ 2 = **15 cm**\n\nJaanch: 2 × f = 2 × 15 cm = 30 cm = R ✅\n\n### Example 2: f se R nikaalo\n\nEk convex mirror ki focal length 12 cm hai. Uski radius of curvature kya hai?\n\n- R = 2 × f = 2 × 12 cm = **24 cm**\n\nJaanch: R ÷ 2 = 24 cm ÷ 2 = 12 cm = f ✅\n\n### Example 3: C aur F ki position\n\nEk concave mirror ki R = 36 cm hai. Pole se F aur C kitni door hain?\n\n- f = R ÷ 2 = 36 cm ÷ 2 = 18 cm\n- F, P se **18 cm** door, mirror ke **aage**.\n- C, P se R = **36 cm** door, mirror ke **aage**.\n- F aur C ke beech ki doori: FC = 36 cm − 18 cm = **18 cm** = PF\n\n```\n C                F               P\n +────────────────+───────────────)\n ◄──── 18 cm ────►◄──── 18 cm ───►\n ◄───────────── 36 cm ───────────►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.12** – Concave mirror: R = 36 cm. PF = 18 cm, FC = 18 cm, PC = 36 cm. P, F aur C teeno mirror ke aage axis par hain (P mirror par).\n\nJaanch: PF + FC = 18 cm + 18 cm = 36 cm = PC ✅\n\nEk convex mirror ki R = 24 cm ho to f = 24 cm ÷ 2 = 12 cm. F aur C, P se **12 cm** aur **24 cm** door hain, dono mirror ke **peeche**.\n\nJaanch: PF = 12 cm, FC = 24 cm − 12 cm = 12 cm, dono barabar ✅\n\n### Example 4: ray dekh kar mirror ka type pehchano\n\n(a) Ek mirror par axis ke parallel kirnein girti hain, aur reflect hone ke baad wo mirror se 8 cm aage ek point par milti hain. Kaunsa mirror hai, aur f aur R kya hain?\n\n- Kirnein sach mein milti hain, point mirror ke aage hai: **concave mirror** (real focus).\n- f = **8 cm**, R = 2 × 8 cm = **16 cm**.\n\n(b) Ek doosre mirror par axis ke parallel kirnein girti hain, aur reflect hone ke baad wo phail jaati hain, jaise mirror ke peeche 6 cm door ek point se aa rahi hon. Kaunsa mirror hai, aur f aur R?\n\n- Kirnein phail rahi hain, aur virtual focus mirror ke peeche hai: **convex mirror**.\n- f = **6 cm**, R = 2 × 6 cm = **12 cm**.\n\nJaanch: (a) 16 cm ÷ 2 = 8 cm ✅   (b) 12 cm ÷ 2 = 6 cm ✅\n\n### Example 5: sun-image activity se f\n\nEk student ne concave mirror se Sooraj ka sabse chamakdaar spot screen par banaya. Mirror aur screen ke beech ki doori 12 cm thi. f aur R?\n\n- f ≈ **12 cm**\n- R = 2 × 12 cm = **24 cm**\n\nJaanch: 24 cm ÷ 2 = 12 cm ✅\n\n### Example 6 (thoda extra): sign convention mein f aur R\n\n(a) Ek concave mirror ki radius 20 cm hai. Sign convention ke saath R aur f likho.\n(b) Ek convex mirror ki radius 40 cm hai. Sign convention ke saath R aur f likho.\n\n(a) Concave mirror ke liye R aur f dono negative hote hain:\n- R = **−20 cm**\n- f = R ÷ 2 = −20 cm ÷ 2 = **−10 cm**\n\n(b) Convex mirror ke liye R aur f dono positive hote hain:\n- R = **+40 cm**\n- f = R ÷ 2 = +40 cm ÷ 2 = **+20 cm**\n\nJaanch: (a) 2 × (−10 cm) = −20 cm = R ✅   (b) 2 × (+20 cm) = +40 cm = R ✅\n\n### Example 7: aperture aur pole pehchano\n\nNeeche ke figure mein ek mirror ko dikhaya gaya hai. A aur B mirror ki surface ke do kinare hain, M mirror ka beech wala point hai, aur Q ek point hai jo M se 20 cm door principal axis par hai. Q mirror ke aage hai. A aur B ke beech ki seedhi doori 4 cm hai, aur Q ko hi mirror ka centre of curvature maanen. (a) Pole kaun sa hai? (b) Aperture kitna hai? (c) R aur f kya hain?\n\n```\n                                 A      ▲\n                                  )     │\n                                   )    │  AB = 4 cm\n Q ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ M)   ┼\n                                   )    │\n                                  )     │\n                                 B      ▼\n ◄─────────── MQ = 20 cm ──────────►\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.13** – Ek concave mirror: A aur B kinare hain, M surface ka beech ka point (pole) hai, aur Q (mirror ke aage, axis par) M se 20 cm door hai.\n\n(a) Surface ka beech wala point hi **pole** hai: **M = P**.\n(b) Aperture = AB = **4 cm**.\n(c) C = Q, isliye R = PC = MQ = **20 cm**, aur f = 20 cm ÷ 2 = **10 cm**.\n\nJaanch: aperture (4 cm) R (20 cm) se kaafi chhota hai, 4 ÷ 20 = 0.2, yaani chhota aperture hi hai ✅ (isliye f = R ÷ 2 lag sakta hai). Aur 2 × 10 cm = 20 cm = R ✅\n\n### Example 8: teen kirnein ka tracing\n\nEk concave mirror ki focal length 10 cm hai. Ek ray principal axis ke parallel hai aur axis se 2 cm upar hai.\n\n(a) Reflect hone ke baad wo kahan jaayegi?\n(b) Ek doosri ray centre of curvature se guzarti hui mirror par girti hai. Wo kahan jaayegi? C kahan hai?\n(c) Teesri ray F se guzar kar mirror par girti hai. Wo kahan jaayegi?\n\n- (a) Axis ke parallel ray reflect hone ke baad **F se guzarti hai**. F pole se **10 cm** door, mirror ke aage hai. To ray principal axis ko P se 10 cm aage kaatti hai.\n- (b) R = 2 × f = 2 × 10 cm = 20 cm. C, P se **20 cm** aage hai. C se guzarti ray normal ki tarah girti hai, isliye **usi raste wapas C ki taraf** lautti hai.\n- (c) F se guzarti ray reflect hone ke baad **principal axis ke parallel** ho jaati hai.\n\nJaanch: f = R ÷ 2 = 20 cm ÷ 2 = 10 cm ✅\n\nAb yahi ray (parallel, axis se 2 cm upar) ek **convex mirror** (f = 10 cm) par aaye to: reflect hone ke baad wo **phail jaayegi**, jaise mirror ke peeche 10 cm door F se aa rahi ho. Reflected ray principal axis ko mirror ke aage kahin nahi kaategi.\n\nJaanch: convex ka F virtual hai, isliye reflected kirnein sach mein kisi point par nahi milti ✅\n\n### Example 9: 10 roz ki surfaces ko sort karo\n\nNeeche 10 cheezein hain. Har ek ko concave, convex ya plane mein daalo.\n\n| Cheez | Type | Kyun |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Chammach ka bowl (andar ka hissa) | Concave | Surface andar ki taraf dabi hai |\n| 2. Chammach ki peeth | Convex | Surface bahar ki taraf ubhri hai |\n| 3. Car ke headlight ka reflector | Concave | Andar ki taraf dabi, katori jaisa |\n| 4. Gaadi ka rear-view mirror | Convex | Bahar ki taraf ubhra hai, bada area dikhata hai |\n| 5. Shaving mirror | Concave | Andar ki taraf dabi hai |\n| 6. Dukaan ka security mirror | Convex | Bahar ki taraf ubhra hai, wide field of view |\n| 7. Steel ki sapaat plate (thali) | Plane | Surface bilkul sapaat hai |\n| 8. Kaanch ki chamakdaar gend (bahar se) | Convex | Bahar ki taraf ubhri hui surface |\n| 9. Karchhi (ladle) | Andar ka hissa concave, peeth convex | Chammach ki tarah, dono side |\n| 10. Dentist ka chhota mirror | Concave | Andar ki taraf dabi, chhota |\n\nYahan sirf **shape pehchanni** hai. In mirrors ke uses ki baat Topic 6 mein.\n\n### Example 10: PF = FC = f ka istemaal\n\n(a) Ek concave mirror mein pole P se centre of curvature C ki doori 50 cm hai. f aur FC kya hain?\n(b) Ek mirror mein PF = 9 cm hai. FC aur PC kya hain?\n\n(a) PC = R = 50 cm, to f = 50 cm ÷ 2 = **25 cm**. FC = PC − PF = 50 cm − 25 cm = **25 cm**.\n(b) PF = FC = 9 cm. PC = PF + FC = 9 cm + 9 cm = **18 cm**.\n\nJaanch: (a) PF = FC = 25 cm ✅   (b) PC = 2 × 9 cm = 18 cm ✅\n\n### Example 11: unit badalna aur ray 4\n\n(a) Ek concave mirror ki radius R = 0.4 m hai. f cm mein kitni hai?\n(b) Ek ray concave mirror ke pole P par principal axis se 30° ka angle bana kar girti hai. Reflected ray axis ke saath kitna angle banayegi, aur incident aur reflected ray ke beech kitna angle hoga?\n\n(a) f = R ÷ 2 = 0.4 m ÷ 2 = 0.2 m = 0.2 × 100 cm = **20 cm**.\n\n(b) Pole par principal axis hi normal hai, isliye ∠i = 30° to ∠r = **30°**, axis ke doosri taraf. Incident aur reflected ray ke beech ka angle = ∠i + ∠r = 30° + 30° = **60°**.\n\nJaanch: (a) 20 cm × 2 = 40 cm = 0.4 m = R ✅   (b) ∠r = ∠i = 30° ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part H: Resolving Maya's Spoon Mystery Completely",
            "headingHinglish": "Part H: Prisha ka sawal, poora jawab",
            "body": "Mr. Miller picked up the soup spoon and turned it slowly before Maya.\n\n\"Now you have the complete scientific explanation for your spoon,\" Mr. Miller said.\n\"When you look into the **bowl of the spoon**, you are gazing into a **concave mirror**. Because your face is held several inches away—well beyond its focal point and centre of curvature—the converging rays cross in front of the bowl, flipping the image **upside down and diminished**.\nWhen you flip the spoon over to look at the **back**, you are gazing into a **convex mirror**. Convex mirrors always diverge light rays, creating a virtual image that is **erect and diminished**, no matter how far away you stand.\"\n\nMaya asked eagerly, \"What happens if I bring the concave bowl very close to my eye, inside its focal length?\"\n\nMr. Miller smiled knowingly. \"That is the thrilling secret we will explore in Topic 5! When an object is moved inside the focal length of a concave mirror, the image suddenly snaps upright and becomes enormously magnified—which is exactly how dental mirrors and makeup mirrors work!\"\n\nMaya brought the spoon bowl right up to her eyelashes and watched in delight as her pupil filled the entire curve of the spoon.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Saluja ne chammach wapas uthaya aur dono side Prisha ko dikhayi.\n\n\"Dekho bitiya, tumhara chehra chammach ke **bowl (concave mirror)** mein chhota aur ulta dikha, aur **peeth (convex mirror)** mein chhota aur seedha. Dono mirror ke **shape alag** hain, isliye kirnein reflect hone ke baad alag tarah se jaati hain. Concave mirror kirnein ko ikattha karta hai (converge), convex unhein failata hai (diverge). Isi se image bhi alag banti hai.\"\n\nPrisha ne poochha, \"Par bowl mein chehra ulta kyun dikha? Aur agar main chammach ko chehre ke bahut kareeb le aaun to?\"\n\nMr. Saluja muskuraye, \"Ye agle topic ka mazedaar hissa hai. Chehra mirror se kitna door hai, ye ginti mein aata hai: kareeb ho ya door, tab image ka size aur seedha-ulta hona badal jaata hai. Pehle tumne mirror, uske points aur kirnein samajh li hain, ab unse image banana seekhna aasaan hoga.\"\n\nPrisha ne chammach ko dheere-dheere apne chehre ke kareeb laaya aur phir door le gayi. Image ko badalte dekh kar wo muskurai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Confusing concave with convex mirrors.** ❌ Remember: *concave* forms a hollow **cave** curving inward (spoon bowl); *convex* bulges outward (spoon back).\n2. **Mistake 2: Measuring the radius of curvature from the focus.** ❌ $R = PC$ is measured strictly **from the pole to the centre of curvature**. The distance from focus to centre is only $FC = R/2$.\n3. **Mistake 3: Writing $f = R$ instead of $f = R/2$.** ❌ The focal length is half the radius. If $R = 40\text{ cm}$, then $f = 20\text{ cm}$, not $40\text{ cm}$.\n4. **Mistake 4: Drawing the focus of a convex mirror in front of the mirror.** ❌ A convex mirror possesses a **virtual focus** situated **behind** the reflective surface. Placing it in front mistakenly turns it into a concave mirror.\n5. **Mistake 5: Confusing the pole with the centre of curvature.** ❌ Pole $P$ is a physical point on the mirror surface; centre $C$ is the geometric origin of the parent sphere located $R$ units away.\n6. **Mistake 6: Assuming concave focal length is positive under Cartesian conventions.** ❌ Because a concave mirror's focus lies in front (to the left of $P$, opposing incoming light), $f$ and $R$ are **strictly negative** (e.g., $f = -10\text{ cm}$).\n7. **Mistake 7: Applying $f = R/2$ to large-aperture mirrors.** ❌ $f = R/2$ is strictly valid for **small apertures** where marginal rays do not cause spherical aberration.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**1. Concave aur convex ko aapas mein ulta bol dena.**\n- Yaad rakho: concave = andar ki taraf dabi (gufa/cave). Chammach ka bowl concave, peeth convex.\n\n**2. R ko focus se naapna.**\n- R = PC, yaani **pole se centre of curvature tak**. Focus se C tak ki doori sirf FC = R ÷ 2 hai.\n- Example: R = 30 cm. Focus se C tak 15 cm hai, 30 cm nahi.\n\n**3. Sochna ki f = R.**\n- Sahi: f = R ÷ 2. Agar R = 40 cm to f = 20 cm, 40 cm nahi.\n- Jaanch: f = 20 cm lene par 2 × 20 cm = 40 cm = R ✅. Agar f = 40 cm maante, to R = 2 × 40 cm = 80 cm banta, jo diya hua R nahi hai ❌.\n\n**4. Convex mirror ka focus mirror ke aage dikhana.**\n- Convex mirror ka focus **virtual** hai aur mirror ke **peeche** hota hai. Aage banaoge to wo ek concave mirror jaisa dikhega.\n\n**5. Centre of curvature ko pole samajhna.**\n- P mirror ki surface par hai, C gole ka centre hai jo mirror ke aage (concave) ya peeche (convex) hota hai. Dono ke beech ki doori R hai, isliye alag-alag hain.\n\n**6. Sochna ki sign convention mein concave ki focal length positive hoti hai.**\n- Concave ka F mirror ke aage, light ki **ulti** direction mein hai, isliye f **negative** hai. Example: R = −20 cm to f = −10 cm.\n\n**7. Bade aperture ke mirror par bhi f = R ÷ 2 laga dena.**\n- Ye result chhote aperture ke liye hai. Bade aperture mein axis se door ki kirnein F par theek se nahi milti."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- A **spherical mirror** is a reflecting surface forming a segment of a hollow sphere.\n- **Concave mirror (converging):** Curves inward; converges parallel rays to a **real focus** in front of the mirror.\n- **Convex mirror (diverging):** Bulges outward; diverges parallel rays from a **virtual focus** behind the mirror.\n- Key landmarks: **Pole ($P$)**, **Centre of Curvature ($C$)**, **Radius of Curvature ($R = PC$)**, **Principal Axis**, **Aperture ($AB$)**, **Principal Focus ($F$)**, **Focal Length ($f = PF$)**.\n- Fundamental geometric relation for small apertures: **$f = R/2$**, meaning $PF = FC = f$ and $R = 2f$.\n- The **Four Standard Rays**:\n  1. Parallel to axis $\to$ reflects through focus $F$ (or appears to diverge from $F$).\n  2. Through focus $F \to$ reflects parallel to principal axis.\n  3. Through centre $C \to$ reflects back along its own path ($\u0007ngle i = 0^\\circ$).\n  4. At pole $P \to$ reflects symmetrically across the principal axis ($\u0007ngle r = \u0007ngle i$).\n- **Cartesian sign convention:** Concave mirror has **negative** $f$ and $R$; convex mirror has **positive** $f$ and $R$.\n- Convex mirrors provide a far wider **field of view** than concave or plane mirrors.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Spherical mirror** = mirror jiski reflecting surface khokhle gole ka hissa ho.\n- **Concave** (converging): andar ki taraf dabi. **Convex** (diverging): bahar ki taraf ubhri.\n- **P** = pole (surface ka beech). **C** = centre of curvature. **R = PC.** **Principal axis** = P aur C se guzarti line. **Aperture** = reflecting surface ki chaudai.\n- **F** = principal focus, **f = PF** = focal length. Concave ka F real (mirror ke aage), convex ka F virtual (mirror ke peeche).\n- **f = R ÷ 2.** PF = FC = f. Ye chhote aperture ke liye. Chhote aperture kyun: baaki kirnein F par theek se nahi milti (spherical aberration, aage ki class mein).\n- **Teen-chaar khaas kirnein:** (1) axis ke parallel: F se guzarti ya F se aati lagti hai. (2) F se guzarti (ya F ki taraf jaati): axis ke parallel. (3) C se guzarti (ya C ki taraf jaati): usi raste wapas. (4) Pole par: ∠i = ∠r, axis ke doosri taraf.\n- **Sign convention (thoda extra, Class 10 overlap):** pole origin hai, light ki direction mein doori +, ulti mein −. Concave ke f aur R negative, convex ke positive.\n- Sun-image activity: mirror se sharp bright spot tak ki doori ≈ f.\n- Convex mirror ka field of view concave se zyada hota hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. A concave mirror has a radius of curvature $R = 18\text{ cm}$. Calculate its focal length. How far from the pole are points $F$ and $C$ located?\n2. A convex mirror has a focal length of $25\text{ cm}$. Determine its radius of curvature. Are $F$ and $C$ located in front of or behind the mirror?\n3. A student focuses sunlight onto a card using a concave mirror, obtaining a razor-sharp pinpoint spot at a distance of $9.5\text{ cm}$ from the mirror. Estimate $f$ and $R$.\n4. *(Class 10 Extension)* Using the Cartesian sign convention (light traveling left to right), write $R$ and $f$ with proper algebraic signs for:\n   - (a) A concave mirror of radius $32\text{ cm}$.\n   - (b) A convex mirror of focal length $12\text{ cm}$.\n5. A parallel beam of light strikes a curved mirror. After reflection, the rays diverge and appear to radiate from a point $15\text{ cm}$ behind the mirror. Identify the mirror type and determine its $f$ and $R$.\n6. State whether each statement is True or False, correcting any false statements:\n   - (a) The pole of a spherical mirror coincides with its centre of curvature.\n   - (b) The focal length equals the radius of curvature ($f = R$) for any spherical mirror.\n   - (c) The principal focus of a convex mirror is a real focus.\n   - (d) Under Cartesian sign conventions, the focal length of a concave mirror is negative.\n7. Explain in a single sentence why small-aperture mirrors are preferred when studying focal length and image formation.\n8. A student states: \"My concave mirror has $R = 40\text{ cm}$, so its focal length is $40\text{ cm}$.\" Identify the conceptual error, provide the correct focal length, and state the position of $F$ relative to $P$ and $C$.\n9. A concave mirror has a focal length $f = 5\text{ cm}$.\n   - (a) Where does a ray traveling parallel to the axis, $1\text{ cm}$ above it, cross the principal axis after reflection?\n   - (b) What is $R$, and what happens to a ray directed through $C$?\n   - (c) A ray strikes the pole at an angle of $25^\\circ$ to the axis. What is the total angle between the incident and reflected rays?\n10. Classify each of the following surfaces as concave, convex, or plane:\n   - (a) The interior bowl of a polished metal ladle.\n   - (b) The exterior back of the same metal ladle.\n   - (c) A flat glass architectural windowpane.\n   - (d) A shiny silver ball-bearing sphere.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. $f = R/2 = 18\text{ cm} / 2 = \\mathbf{9\text{ cm}}$. Focus $F$ is $\\mathbf{9\text{ cm}}$ from the pole, and centre $C$ is $\\mathbf{18\text{ cm}}$ from the pole (both in front of the mirror).\n   *Verification:* $2 \times 9\text{ cm} = 18\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n2. $R = 2f = 2 \times 25\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{50\text{ cm}}$. Both $F$ and $C$ lie **behind the mirror**.\n   *Verification:* $50 / 2 = 25\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n3. $f \u0007pprox \\mathbf{9.5\text{ cm}}$, $R = 2 \times 9.5\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{19.0\text{ cm}}$.\n   *Verification:* $19.0 / 2 = 9.5\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n4. (a) Concave: $R = \\mathbf{-32\text{ cm}}$, $f = -32/2 = \\mathbf{-16\text{ cm}}$.\n   (b) Convex: $f = \\mathbf{+12\text{ cm}}$, $R = 2 \times (+12) = \\mathbf{+24\text{ cm}}$.\n   *Verification:* $2 \times (-16) = -32\text{ cm}$ and $24/2 = 12\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n5. Because rays diverge from a point behind the mirror, it is a **convex mirror**. $f = \\mathbf{15\text{ cm}}$ and $R = 2 \times 15\text{ cm} = \\mathbf{30\text{ cm}}$.\n   *Verification:* $30 / 2 = 15\text{ cm} \\; \\checkmark$\n\n6. (a) **False** ❌. The pole lies on the physical mirror surface, whereas the centre of curvature is the center of the sphere ($PC = R$).\n   (b) **False** ❌. The correct relation is $f = R/2$.\n   (c) **False** ❌. A convex mirror has a virtual focus located behind the mirror.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Focus $F$ lies against the direction of incident light, so $f < 0$.\n\n7. Small apertures ensure that all rays strike close to the principal axis (paraxial rays), eliminating spherical aberration and converging cleanly at a single sharp focal point.\n\n8. The error is confusing radius with focal length. The correct focal length is $f = R/2 = 40/2 = \\mathbf{20\text{ cm}}$. Point $F$ lies **exactly midway between $P$ and $C$** ($PF = FC = 20\text{ cm}$).\n   *Verification:* $PF + FC = 20 + 20 = 40\text{ cm} = PC \\; \\checkmark$\n\n9. (a) The ray passes through focus $F$, crossing the axis at $\\mathbf{5\text{ cm}}$ in front of pole $P$.\n   (b) $R = 2f = 2 \times 5 = \\mathbf{10\text{ cm}}$. A ray through $C$ strikes normally ($\u0007ngle i = 0^\\circ$) and rebounds straight back along its incoming path.\n   (c) $\u0007ngle i = 25^\\circ \\implies \u0007ngle r = 25^\\circ$. Total angle $\theta = 25^\\circ + 25^\\circ = \\mathbf{50^\\circ}$.\n   *Verification:* $5\text{ cm} = f$, $10/2 = 5\text{ cm}$, and $\u0007ngle i = \u0007ngle r = 25^\\circ \\; \\checkmark$\n\n10. (a) Ladle interior: **Concave** (curving inward).\n    (b) Ladle exterior: **Convex** (bulging outward).\n    (c) Windowpane: **Plane** (flat optical surface).\n    (d) Ball-bearing: **Convex** (exterior of a polished sphere).",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. A concave mirror has a radius of curvature R = 18 cm. Find its focal length. How far from the pole are F and C?\n2. A convex mirror has a focal length of 25 cm. Find its radius of curvature. Are F and C in front of or behind the mirror?\n3. A student focuses the Sun's image on a screen using a concave mirror. The sharp bright spot is 9.5 cm from the mirror. Estimate f and R.\n4. *(Thoda extra)* Using the sign convention (pole as origin, light travelling left to right), write R and f (with signs) for (a) a concave mirror of radius 32 cm and (b) a convex mirror of focal length 12 cm.\n5. A parallel beam falls on a mirror. After reflection the rays spread out and appear to come from a point 15 cm behind the mirror. Which type of mirror is it? Find f and R.\n6. Say true or false and correct if false: (a) The pole of a mirror is also its centre of curvature. (b) f = R for any spherical mirror. (c) The focus of a convex mirror is real. (d) In the sign convention, the focal length of a concave mirror is negative.\n7. Why do we use mirrors of small aperture when we study focal length? Give a one-line reason.\n8. A student says, \"My concave mirror has R = 40 cm so its focal length is 40 cm.\" Point out the mistake and give the correct answer. Also tell where F lies between P and C.\n9. A concave mirror has f = 5 cm. (a) Where does a ray parallel to the axis, 1 cm above it, cross the principal axis after reflection? (b) What is R, and what happens to a ray directed through C? (c) A ray falls at the pole at 25° to the axis. What is the angle between the incident and the reflected ray?\n10. Classify as concave, convex or plane: (a) the inside of a small steel bowl, (b) the outside of the same bowl, (c) a flat steel plate, (d) a polished steel ball.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. f = R ÷ 2 = 18 cm ÷ 2 = **9 cm**. F, P se **9 cm** aur C, P se **18 cm** door hain (dono mirror ke aage).\n   Jaanch: 2 × 9 cm = 18 cm ✅\n\n2. R = 2 × f = 2 × 25 cm = **50 cm**. Convex mirror mein F aur C dono mirror ke **peeche** hain.\n   Jaanch: 50 cm ÷ 2 = 25 cm ✅\n\n3. f ≈ **9.5 cm**, R = 2 × 9.5 cm = **19 cm**.\n   Jaanch: 19 cm ÷ 2 = 9.5 cm ✅\n\n4. (a) Concave: R = **−32 cm**, f = −32 cm ÷ 2 = **−16 cm**.\n   (b) Convex: f = **+12 cm**, R = 2 × (+12 cm) = **+24 cm**.\n   Jaanch: (a) 2 × (−16 cm) = −32 cm ✅   (b) +24 cm ÷ 2 = +12 cm ✅\n\n5. Kirnein phail rahi hain aur virtual focus mirror ke peeche hai, isliye **convex mirror**. f = **15 cm**, R = 2 × 15 cm = **30 cm**.\n   Jaanch: 30 cm ÷ 2 = 15 cm ✅\n\n6. (a) **False** ❌. Pole mirror par hota hai, centre of curvature gole ka centre hai (PC = R).\n   (b) **False** ❌. f = R ÷ 2.\n   (c) **False** ❌. Convex mirror ka focus virtual hai, mirror ke peeche.\n   (d) **True** ✅. F light ki ulti direction mein hai, isliye f negative.\n\n7. Bade aperture mein axis se door ki kirnein F par theek se nahi milti, isliye focus saaf nahi banta. Chhote aperture mein saari kirnein lagbhag ek hi point F par milti hain.\n\n8. Galti: f = R samajhna. Sahi: f = R ÷ 2 = 40 cm ÷ 2 = **20 cm**. F pole P se 20 cm door hai, yaani P aur C ke **theek beech mein** (FC = 40 cm − 20 cm = 20 cm).\n   Jaanch: PF = FC = 20 cm ✅\n\n9. (a) Ray F se guzarti hai, isliye axis ko P se **5 cm** aage kaatti hai.\n   (b) R = 2 × 5 cm = **10 cm**. C se guzarti ray normal ki tarah girti hai aur **usi raste wapas** lautti hai.\n   (c) ∠r = ∠i = 25°, to beech ka angle = 25° + 25° = **50°**.\n   Jaanch: (a) 5 cm = f ✅   (b) 10 cm ÷ 2 = 5 cm ✅   (c) 25° + 25° = 50° ✅\n\n10. (a) **Concave** (andar ki taraf dabi). (b) **Convex** (bahar ki taraf ubhri). (c) **Plane** (sapaat). (d) **Convex** (gole ki bahar ki surface).\n\n---\n\nAb tum pehchan sakte ho ki mirror concave hai ya convex, uske P, C, F, f aur R kya hain, aur f = R ÷ 2 se ek se doosra nikaal sakte ho. Chammach ke dono side ke raaz ka pehla hissa khul gaya: dono alag mirror hain.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab dekhte hain ki object ko mirror ke kareeb ya door rakhne par image kaisi banti hai. Topic 5 mein hum in teen-chaar khaas kirnein se ray diagram banayenge aur dekhenge ki Prisha ka chehra bowl mein ulta aur peeth mein seedha kyun dikha."
          }
